<h3>How was our face mask dataset created?</h3>



<p>Prajna, like me, has been feeling down and depressed about the state of =
the world =E2=80=94 thousands of people are dying each day, and for many of=
 us, there is very little (if anything) we can do.</p>



<p>To help keep her spirts up, Prajna decided to distract herself by applyi=
ng computer vision and deep learning to solve a real-world problem:</p>



<ul><li>Best case scenario =E2=80=94 she could use her project to help othe=
rs</li><li>Worst case scenario =E2=80=94 it gave her a much needed mental e=
scape</li></ul>



<p><strong>Either way, it=E2=80=99s win-win!</strong></p>



<p>As programmers, developers, and computer vision/deep learning practition=
ers, we can <em>all</em> take a page from Prajna=E2=80=99s book =E2=80=94 l=
et your skills become your distraction and your haven.</p>



<p>To create this dataset, Prajna had the ingenious solution of:</p>



<ol><li>Taking <em>normal images of faces</em></li><li>Then creating a <em>=
custom computer vision Python script</em> to add face masks to them, <stron=
g>thereby creating an <em>artificial</em> (but still real-world applicable)=
 dataset</strong></li></ol>



<p>This method is actually a lot easier than it sounds once you <a href=3D"=
https://www.pyimagesearch.com/2017/04/03/facial-landmarks-dlib-opencv-pytho=
n/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noreferrer noopener" aria-label=3D" (opens in =
a new tab)">apply facial landmarks to the problem</a>.</p>



<p>Facial landmarks allow us to automatically infer the location of facial =
structures, including:</p>



<ul><li>Eyes</li><li>Eyebrows</li><li>Nose</li><li>Mouth</li><li>Jawline</l=
i></ul>



<p>To use facial landmarks to build a dataset of faces wearing face masks, =
we need to first start with an image of a person <em>not</em> wearing a fac=
e mask:</p>



<div class=3D"wp-block-image"><figure class=3D"aligncenter size-large"><img=
